# UX principles

Every page should help a Temple student make one decision quickly. Say less, and make what's left easy to scan.

## Keep pages light
- One clear purpose per page and per section. If a section needs a paragraph to explain itself, cut or split it.
- Lead with what the student needs most (price, walk time, room type). Put details behind a link, a listing page, or a disclosure.
- Prefer short sentences and plain words. Aim for 1–2 sentences of supporting copy per section.
- Don't add stats, badges, or callouts unless they change what a student would do. Remove any that don't.
- Group related controls: one dropdown beats a row of many buttons (e.g. the star-rating filter on /reviews).

## Design requests are not feature requests
- "Make it look better / less tacky / more organized" means restyle what exists: spacing, hierarchy, alignment, color, grouping.
- Don't add new sections, charts, or features unless explicitly asked. If a new feature seems useful, suggest it instead of building it.

## Consistency
- Reuse existing components and patterns (cards, section headings, native selects styled like the pricing calculator).
- Same spacing rhythm between sections; same heading style on every page.
- Keep Temple cherry (`--primary`) identical in light and dark mode. Check both modes.

## Clarity and trust
- Only show facts from official or listed sources; never invent numbers. Label estimates as estimates.
- Photos must be real and freely licensed (see `src/data/photos.ts`), credited where shown. Never use AI-generated or unrelated photos for a specific building. Listings without a photo show Google's Street View embed (`src/data/street-views.ts`); never download or re-host Google imagery, and keep Google's credits visible.
- Explain a number in place when it could mislead (e.g. "whole apartment" vs per-bedroom rent).
- Destructive actions (delete) need a confirm step. Empty states say what to do next.

## Quality floor
- Works at phone width with no sideways scrolling.
- Visible focus states, real labels on inputs, readable contrast.
- Verify UI changes in the browser before calling them done.
